 - **Popover.** `components/ui/popover.tsx` meets WCAG 1.4.13. It opens on pointer hover after 150 ms, on keyboard focus of the badge, and on click or tap (touch has no hover); the focus a pointer press brings opens nothing by itself (the click decides), and a press that ends without a click, such as a touch that turned into a scroll, leaves keyboard focus working. Hover (the pointer over the badge or the popover, so the pointer can move into it) and focus (keyboard focus on the badge, or focus inside the popover; the focus a click leaves on the badge does not count) are tracked separately, and an open popover stays while either holds: it closes 200 ms after the pointer has left both while focus is elsewhere, or when focus leaves both while the pointer is elsewhere. A click pins it: a pinned popover ignores hover and closes when focus moves on to another control, and a second click closes it. It also closes on Esc (focus returns to the badge when it was inside; a dialog around it stays open), on a press outside, and when its badge has left the viewport (the window shrank, say). It is a non-modal `role="dialog"` named "Issues on <id>", rendered in the top layer with the native `popover` attribute (`showPopover()`), so no node, panel, or dialog covers it and the canvas zoom does not scale it. It sits right after its badge in the DOM, so Tab moves from the badge into its rows and on out; ArrowDown on the badge moves in, and the arrow keys, Home, and End move between rows. It is placed from the badge's box with JavaScript: below it, or above when below has no room (never over the badge), shifted to stay inside the viewport, and scrolling when taller than the room; it is placed again on resize, on a scroll around it, and on content changes. A pan, a zoom, or a node drag closes the canvas's popovers (`closePopovers(scope)`), and opening one closes any other. There is no animation, so reduced motion needs nothing. A browser without the popover API gets a fixed-position element portalled to `document.body` while open, out of the canvas's transformed nodes, with the keyboard order kept by hand (Tab from the badge to the first row, Shift+Tab from the first row back to the badge, Tab past the last row on to what follows the badge, where the usual rules decide whether it stays, and Shift+Tab from there back to the last row); inside the node editor, a modal dialog that makes the rest of the page inert, it stays in place.
 - **Rows.** Each issue shows its severity as an icon and a word (Error or Warning), its code, its message, and its field path in `<code>`. Choosing one opens the node's editor with the control at that path focused: `openNode(id, { field })` leaves a pending target in the store that the dialog uses once, and `editor/focus-field.ts` finds the first control inside the element whose `data-field` equals the path (config paths in the config form, `id` and `label` among the node's own fields), trying the parent paths, else the dialog's heading. Paths come from user data (a record key may hold a quote, a backslash, or a newline), so the values are compared as strings, never built into a selector. An issue without a path opens the node with focus on the heading. A `FIELD_INPUT_INVALID` row uses the control's noun: **Discard the unparsed text** or **Discard the incomplete schedule**; when that removes the last issue, and with it the badge, focus goes to the node card (or the dialog's heading) rather than the page. Since #14, `focusField` first opens any collapsed Advanced group or list item that holds the field.
 - **In the node editor.** The same badge sits beside the dialog's title (the `Dialog` primitive's `actions` slot), so the issues are at hand while editing; choosing one focuses its field in place. The form's own Config issues summary is unchanged.
-- **Beside Publish.** `editor/ValidationIndicator.tsx`, left of Publish, is a status, not a button, when there is nothing to list: "Ready to publish" once the API's checks of the saved draft (`POST /loops/{id}/validate`: cron syntax, subloop references, the model and classifier catalogs; they run for the copy the server holds, keyed as "Freshness" under the classifier models says) have answered under a resolved model and classifier catalog state with nothing to fix, "Checking…" while the catalogs or their check are under way, and "Validation unavailable" (muted; a failed catalog lookup holds Publish even when cached choices remain, while Publish can retry a failed server check) when they could not run, offline say. Otherwise it is a button named by the counts ("2 errors, 1 warning", from the same merged list Publish checks) with the same popover, listing every loop-level issue, every edge issue, and any issue about a node no longer in the draft in full, then one row per node with issues ("prep: 2 issues") that opens that node. Edge rows only inform: the store does not select edges. Every issue appears in exactly one place, so the node badges and the loop rows add up to the counts. Publish refuses with the same messages as before, and keeps its tooltip about blocking errors.
+- **Beside Publish.** `editor/ValidationIndicator.tsx`, left of Publish, is a status, not a button, when there is nothing to list: "Ready to publish" once the API's checks of the saved draft (`POST /loops/{id}/validate`: cron syntax, subloop references, the model and classifier catalogs; they run for the copy the server holds, keyed as "Freshness" under the classifier models says) have answered under a resolved model and classifier catalog state with nothing to fix, "Checking…" while the catalogs or their check are under way, and "Validation unavailable" (muted; a failed catalog lookup holds Publish even when cached choices remain, while Publish can retry a failed server check) when they could not run, offline say, or cannot run because a draft conflict or a name refusal keeps the draft from the server (its tooltip is then Publish's refusal; see "Publishing and the Loops list (#237)"). Otherwise it is a button named by the counts ("2 errors, 1 warning", from the same merged list Publish checks) with the same popover, listing every loop-level issue, every edge issue, and any issue about a node no longer in the draft in full, then one row per node with issues ("prep: 2 issues") that opens that node. Edge rows only inform: the store does not select edges. Every issue appears in exactly one place, so the node badges and the loop rows add up to the counts. Publish refuses with the same messages as before, and keeps its tooltip about blocking errors.
 - **Removed.** The validation section under the loop panel, the counts on its collapsed rail, and the node editor's issue list.
 - **Rendering.** Canvas nodes receive their issues (not a count) in their data; a node whose definition and issues did not change keeps its data object, and the memoised card does not re-render.
 - **Contrast.** The badge edges and the focus ring on every node band, and the popover's severity chips, are in the contrast table; the popover uses the overlay surface, a default border, and `shadow-3`.
